CRON(8)                        P32W Unix                         CRON(8)

NAME
       cron - clock daemon

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/cron

DESCRIPTION
       cron runs commands at set times. /etc/rc starts it at boot;
       it puts itself in the background at once (the parent
       returns, so /etc/rc goes on), leaves its terminal, ignores
       SIGINT, SIGQUIT and SIGHUP, and connects its standard input
       and output to /dev/null. Only root may run it.

       cron wakes at the start of each minute and then does two
       things:

       1.  It reads /etc/crontab (see crontab(5)) and runs the
           command of every line that matches the minute, in local
           time (TZ, see tz(5)). As POSIX says, when both the
           day-of-month and the day-of-week fields are restricted
           (neither is *), a day matching either one will do;
           otherwise the day must match both. The file is read
           afresh each
           minute, so an edit takes effect at the next minute
           without a restart.

       2.  It runs the jobs at(1) has queued in /usr/spool/at whose
           time has come, at most 32 a minute (the rest wait for
           the next minute).

       A crontab command runs as root, in /, by sh -c command, with
       its standard input, output and error on /dev/null unless the
       command redirects them, umask 022, and the environment
       HOME=/, USER=root, LOGNAME=root, PATH=/bin:/usr/bin and
       SHELL=/bin/sh (and cron's TZ, if it has one).

       An at job runs as the owner of its spool file (whatever the
       file's first line says), with that user's group from
       /etc/passwd, in the directory recorded when it was queued
       (if it cannot change there, the job is not run and the
       reason goes to at.out), with the umask and the environment
       variables at saved ("# umask" and "# env" lines; see at(1)).
       HOME, USER, LOGNAME and SHELL that were not saved come from
       the user's /etc/passwd entry, PATH is /bin:/usr/bin and TZ
       is cron's. Its output is appended to at.out in the user's
       home directory. The spool file is renamed to /tmp/atNNN and
       run from there by sh, and the copy is removed when the job
       has finished; a job whose owner is not in /etc/passwd, or
       whose first line is not "# at uid gid dir", is deleted
       without being run.

       cron does not wait for the commands it starts: each runs as
       a grandchild, so a long job does not delay the next minute.

EXIT STATUS
       0 when it has put itself in the background; 1 if not run by
       root or it cannot fork.

FILES
       /etc/crontab   the table of commands
       /usr/spool/at  the at queue
       /tmp/at*       at jobs while they run
       $HOME/at.out   the output of a user's at jobs

SEE ALSO
       crontab(5), at(1), rc(8), sh(1), tz(5)

BUGS
       Nothing is logged, and the output of crontab commands is
       thrown away; redirect it in the command.

       There are no per-user crontabs and no crontab(1) command:
       edit /etc/crontab as root.

       Minutes are not made up: if the machine is down, or cron is
       late, the crontab lines of a missed minute are not run (at
       jobs are: they run at the next minute cron sees).

       A job copy is left in /tmp if the machine stops while the
       job runs.
